  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 143 AM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nSaturday/Saturday night\nAssessment...a certainty (>95%) of heat and humidity\n\nThe impactful heat and humidity will continue through Saturday. \nAreas north of I-80 will see peak afternoon heat indices of 95-100. \nAlong the I-80 corridor and south, peak afternoon heat indices will \nbe over 100 again. The extreme heat warning may need to be extended \ninto the I-80 corridor east of the Quad Cities.\n\nWhile an isolated rogue shower or storm cannot be ruled out, \nespecially north of I-80. The first part of the holiday weekend \nlooks to be dry.\n\nSunday through Thursday\nAssessment...very high (>80%) confidence on above normal\ntemperatures.\n\nThe upper level ridge retrogrades briefly into the Plains for the \nsecond half of the holiday weekend but will then push back east for \nthe middle of next week. Thus after a brief reprieve from the heat \nSunday/Monday, heat and humidity will surge back into the area \nTuesday/Wednesday with heat indices approaching 100.\n\nBy late next week the overall flow pattern aloft de-amplifies \nslightly. This will allow temperatures to be closer to but still \nabove normal.\n\nAny rain chances the first half of next week are low (10-25%) and \nare confined to areas along/north of I-80. Thus there will be many \nhours of dry weather.\n\nThe models are attempting to bring a front into the area Tuesday \nnight into Wednesday with the front progged to stall and wash out \nacross the area. The proximity of the front has the model consensus \ngiving slightly higher rain chances (30-50%) for late Tuesday night.\n\nOnce the front stalls and washes out across the area, its remnants \nwould then provide the focus for rain chances the remainder of the \nweek. However, even with a near daily chance of rain, the rain \nchances remain low (20-30%) with the better forcing remaining across \nthe northern Plains and upper Midwest.",
